WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.876
Welcome to À LA FRENCH.
In this episode,

00:00:01.888 --> 00:00:03.820
we welcome our last
guest of the season.

00:00:04.144 --> 00:00:05.880
And this guest is a pioneer.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.580
He is the leader. He is the final boss.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.256
The final boss of AI as we know it today.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.320
He is creating today a
global platform.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.960
He does World Models.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.190
And this guest, we will discuss with
him for an hour and a half.

00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:22.040
And exclusively in
À LA FRENCH, let's go!

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:45.710
Important announcement.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.760
When we announced the
À LA FRENCH evening on June 12,

00:00:47.784 --> 00:00:50.290
in less than 24 hours,
the 250 places were sold.

00:00:50.389 --> 00:00:52.356
So we had a great
evening, but hundreds of

00:00:52.368 --> 00:00:54.666
people couldn't come
and were on a waiting list.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:55.976
And we don't like frustration.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.270
So we decided to do it again, but bigger, for the À LA FRENCH conference.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.350
On December 3, we'll see you there.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:04.268
We will be 1200, coming
from all over France to celebrate

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.870
French tech and a certain vision of tech.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:08.266
We are a nation of engineers.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:11.130
We invented Minitel before
the Internet, the TGV before Hyperloops,

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:14.350
DivX, X264, VLC, before
streaming on Netflix.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:16.950
We are not going to let anyone tell us
how we do tech, are we?

00:01:16.951 --> 00:01:20.111
Come on, the conference that has a certain idea of technology, will be on December 3rd.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.692
One day for the YouTube show community, announcement

00:01:22.704 --> 00:01:24.990
preview, guest
rare, gifts, games.

00:01:25.354 --> 00:01:26.730
And pay attention, this is going to be very French.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.601
This is going to be the most incredible tech conference

00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:29.986
to which you
have never attended.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.250
And we will celebrate together
a certain idea of technology.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.246
You can go to
alafrench.org right now,

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.790
reserve your seats with reduced rates for those who cannot afford it

00:01:36.791 --> 00:01:38.630
and support rates for those who can.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:40.750
So we will meet on December 3rd live.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:42.690
And in the meantime, we're going back to the episode.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:51.660
We are in À LA FRENCH
and you know the game.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.540
To guess the guest, in 4 points,
if you know it immediately,

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.116
3 points if you need hints, 2

00:01:56.128 --> 00:02:00.500
points if you need more hints,
and 1 point when everything has already been said.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.440
Are you ready to play, Steve?

00:02:02.441 --> 00:02:02.840
I'm ready.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:03.600
Ready for what?

00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:04.000
Me, I'm going to win.

00:02:04.001 --> 00:02:04.180
Okay, let's go.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:05.480
So, in 4 points.

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:08.180
Our guest is an
extremely well-known researcher.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:08.759
No, we're joking.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:09.840
It's Yann LeCun.

00:02:16.905 --> 00:02:18.170
Okay, we won't waste any time.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:18.650
Welcome, Yann.

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:19.550
It's a pleasure to be here.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:20.190
Thank you for inviting me.

00:02:20.394 --> 00:02:22.354
In addition, Neon Noir is
a place you know.

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:23.990
You come here from time to time, when you come to France.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.370
Yes, 2-3 times, I've already been here.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:26.610
It's changed a lot.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.750
It's become populated, furnished, expanded.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.770
You know with Yann
for longer than we do.

00:02:33.771 --> 00:02:37.090
Since the very beginning of ZML.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.990
We were introduced by Gabriel.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.250
By Gabriel Synnaeve.

00:02:42.725 --> 00:02:47.270
And then, for the story, we got into
it, got into it in the sense... "Heyyyy !"

00:02:47.271 --> 00:02:48.830
We got into a fight.

00:02:49.505 --> 00:02:51.585
We got into a fight about
programming languages.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:51.950
Yes.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.830
And in particular, what is called multi-stage programming.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.430
So, referring to a language called Terra,

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:02.010
which was a kind of super set of Lua.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:02.610
Yes.

00:03:02.969 --> 00:03:05.510
So, the idea is to have
metaprogramming in the language.

00:03:06.015 --> 00:03:07.815
You see, actually, you have only one control flow.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.530
where you program the language
using the language itself.

00:03:10.555 --> 00:03:13.550
In contrast to a macro, or a template, or all metaprogramming.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.110
And so, he had told me... "ah!!"

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.310
And I see you too, Yann,
every time,

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:20.710
because whenever Steve makes
a very Frenchmaxing tweet,

00:03:21.085 --> 00:03:24.102
with raclette or
things like that, in

00:03:24.182 --> 00:03:27.090
Savoie, all of that, there's a
little like from Yann LeCun.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:28.130
That's kind of it.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:29.150
I can always count on it.

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:30.550
At first, I was happy.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:31.150
I looked.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:31.930
Now, I said yes.

00:03:31.931 --> 00:03:33.550
Is that, the French Maxing?

00:03:33.551 --> 00:03:35.100
Frenchmaxxing, it's nothing now.

00:03:35.124 --> 00:03:36.490
It's a maximization of France.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:37.230
Maximization.

00:03:37.825 --> 00:03:39.786
As Victor Hugo said, who
used, I don't know what word,

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.446
then someone told him: "But
that's not a French word."

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.630
He said, "Well, now, if."

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.650
It's still cool to be called
Hugo when you can define what.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:49.746
The dictionary, it's me.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.270
But this discussion, the reason
for which we had discussed this,

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.410
is because I
am a big fan of LISP.

00:03:55.859 --> 00:03:57.890
And LISP, it's a
language... Exactly.

00:03:58.099 --> 00:03:59.099
Data is code.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:03.370
Yes, data is code, and
then you can tinker.

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:05.370
It's a
programmable programming language.

00:04:05.699 --> 00:04:07.539
There are a lot of parentheses
that we're going to use.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.490
That's not a problem.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.586
Me, I've never been able to get used to it.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:11.850
Is that true?

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:13.066
I've done a lot of closures.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.650
Were you an Emacs guy?

00:04:14.651 --> 00:04:17.870
No, but in Emacs, I was a customer.

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:19.390
I wasn't a programmer at Emacs.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.410
However, when I did a lot of Clojure, which I love,

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.234
in fact, pushed by someone
who is called Pierre-Yves Ritschard

00:04:26.259 --> 00:04:28.310
who is the CTO
of Poolside, now.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.950
I really felt like I was creating a language, you see, a language of the future.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:36.426
Finally, somewhere in the past,
since syntax is all that matters.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:36.890
It's a bit in the past.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:38.466
But damn, I've never managed to get it right.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.031
Parentheses, they make me... I did everything, the colorings, the gadgets and all that.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.030
I've never managed to get it right.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:45.610
And a little known fact,

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:51.690
in 87, when I finished my doctorate,

00:04:52.415 --> 00:04:55.550
I met a young student from polytechnique called Léon Botou.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:57.070
Oh!

00:04:57.071 --> 00:05:02.270
And then, he said, I would like to do a
project for my final year at Polytechnique.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:03.730
Will I
do an internship with you?

00:05:03.731 --> 00:05:05.501
I told him, you can't, because
I haven't finished my thesis.

00:05:05.525 --> 00:05:07.326
It's not a problem, I
won't tell the school.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:11.426
And I told him, well,
listen, I'm starting to write

00:05:11.438 --> 00:05:15.350
a new neural network simulator.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:16.750
Do you want to help me?

00:05:16.751 --> 00:05:19.410
And I said, I would really like
to have an interface language.

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:25.650
I thought about using
Forth and things like that.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.111
Instead of inventing a language,
it might actually be a LISP.

00:05:29.135 --> 00:05:31.470
So, I would really like a LISP interpreter.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.690
And we both had Amiga 1000s.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.550
I see that you don't have
one on your table here.

00:05:36.551 --> 00:05:37.551
But that's a shame.

00:05:37.815 --> 00:05:40.490
Yes, next time, that will be fixed.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.946
But anyway, I didn't insult
you, I brought you a BeBox.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:43.510
A BeBox.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:46.950
But actually, the BeBox
is a copy of the Amiga.

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:48.490
Exactly.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:49.550
There you go.

00:05:49.635 --> 00:05:51.090
And you did it on purpose, of course.

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:54.746
So no, but we have a
conversation on Twitter, actually.

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:56.910
Yann had bounced back
and actually, he had explained.

00:05:56.911 --> 00:05:59.710
Maybe it's a bit difficult to
find Amigas today, right?

00:05:59.711 --> 00:06:01.250
Less than BeBoxes, I
can tell you. That one...

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:04.010
You can find emulators and all that.

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:05.590
But you can also buy them.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:09.744
And Léon, in three weeks,
wrote a tablet-list

00:06:09.756 --> 00:06:12.890
in C, which I used
as a front-end.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.070
Then, I went to do my postdoc in Toronto.

00:06:15.225 --> 00:06:17.528
And actually, we used that system, which

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.910
initially was called SN,
Simulateur Neuronal,

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.050
which later became Lush,
when we open-sourced it in 2002.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:26.267
And I used it in all my research with my

00:06:26.347 --> 00:06:28.830
students until 2011,
where then we switched to Torch.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:30.830
In Lua, so.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:35.010
So, it was based on Lua and then,
PyTorch, but that was much later.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:37.570
So, there was a long lifespan.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:39.190
There were successive versions.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:41.270
What were we following?

00:06:41.271 --> 00:06:45.630
It was written about backpropagation, multi-layer networks, etc.

00:06:45.631 --> 00:06:49.270
justement, the backpropagation of the gradient, is a bit the foundation

00:06:49.271 --> 00:06:51.475
of how today,
the learning engine

00:06:51.487 --> 00:06:53.750
neural networks
is a bit based on that.

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:57.750
Already, back then, your thesis, it's about the
core of neural networks.

00:06:58.035 --> 00:07:01.590
So, what is it?
Is it 87, the end of the thesis?

00:07:01.591 --> 00:07:04.410
For the few Padawans who haven't
yet... Are you born?

00:07:04.411 --> 00:07:07.130
Just, just, just.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:09.710
But then, you go to Bell Labs, right?

00:07:09.711 --> 00:07:13.150
No, first, I do a postdoc at the
University of Toronto with Jeff Hinton.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:14.210
Ah yes, okay.

00:07:14.211 --> 00:07:17.210
No, and after, I went
to Bell Labs, in late 88.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.050
Okay, you stay there for 15
years, right, approximately?

00:07:20.051 --> 00:07:22.030
So, 12 years at AT&amp;T.

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:24.810
So, Bell Labs is the
research lab of AT&amp;T.

00:07:24.994 --> 00:07:28.690
It later changed its name because
AT&amp;T split up, anyway,

00:07:28.885 --> 00:07:30.770
but about a dozen years.

00:07:30.771 --> 00:07:32.531
And it is there that you
really mature and that you

00:07:32.543 --> 00:07:34.770
develop convolutional neural networks, what?

00:07:34.771 --> 00:07:35.410
Really, is this the year?

00:07:35.411 --> 00:07:37.371
Yes, yes.
Finally, I had already started in Toronto.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:42.030
And even in my thesis, actually, I had networks with local connections and all that.

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:43.470
So, it was already a bit there.

00:07:43.471 --> 00:07:46.490
But yes, that's where we
actually did it.

00:07:46.855 --> 00:07:50.490
And then, Léon, on his side,
was doing a thesis in France.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:53.210
And he made it work for
speech recognition.

00:07:53.775 --> 00:07:55.790
He also worked on
convolutional networks.

00:07:56.790 --> 00:08:03.290
And we ended up making products, in fact, that used convolutional networks

00:08:03.291 --> 00:08:05.610
for reading checks,
things like that.

00:08:05.975 --> 00:08:08.990
The applications... Yes,
for letters, addresses.

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:12.130
Yes, addresses, it has
never been a commercial thing.

00:08:12.131 --> 00:08:12.790
It was a prototype.

00:08:12.791 --> 00:08:13.791
Ah yes? Ah okay.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.225
But the commercial applications,
the main one,

00:08:16.237 --> 00:08:18.630
was for reading
amounts on checks

00:08:18.631 --> 00:08:20.970
and things like
that, financial documents.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:23.675
Yes, reports on
you, really, where we do

00:08:23.687 --> 00:08:25.786
the recognition of alphanumeric signs
and everything.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:27.250
It's really for checks.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:30.562
And that had a commercial
life between the

00:08:30.574 --> 00:08:33.650
mid-1990s and
early 2000s.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:35.496
But I was no longer involved.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.789
Because from 1996,
in fact, AT&amp;T separated.

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:43.230
And then, it was the early
years of the Internet.

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:46.717
And the part of AT&amp;T that
commercialized these systems

00:08:46.729 --> 00:08:49.230
of reading checks, in
fact, was separated from us.

00:08:49.879 --> 00:08:52.750
And the patents belonged to them.

00:08:53.499 --> 00:08:56.070
And where, physically?

00:08:56.071 --> 00:08:57.071
In New Jersey.

00:08:57.889 --> 00:08:59.983
And so, you stayed
in New Jersey,

00:08:59.995 --> 00:09:02.470
2003-2013, is that right, New
York University, NYU?

00:09:02.471 --> 00:09:06.229
I spent a short time
at the NEC Research

00:09:06.241 --> 00:09:09.870
Institute, in the large Japanese
company, NEC.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:12.430
And then, that lab, it kind of fell apart.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:16.750
So, I left pretty quickly and I
became a professor at NYU in 2003.

00:09:17.695 --> 00:09:21.870
And ten years later,
2013, I joined Facebook.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:23.806
But part-time,
I was still a professor.

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:25.750
I am still a professor at NYU.

00:09:26.504 --> 00:09:29.330
Actually, it's quite interesting
because the world of academic research

00:09:29.331 --> 00:09:31.811
and the world of research
in the company are quite linked.

00:09:31.874 --> 00:09:36.450
In any case, and me, sometimes, I think,
is this the world, the open source,

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.276
or in any case, the free sharing
of information, of research,

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:41.670
is this not what
drives, what sometimes obliges companies

00:09:41.671 --> 00:09:45.210
to keep a foot in publications
or in this kind of things?

00:09:45.211 --> 00:09:48.111
No, in fact, what drives in
large part… No, the direct, the bar.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:53.446
What drives companies in
large part to have relationships with the university,

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.550
in any case, in the United States,
is access to talent.

00:09:56.235 --> 00:09:59.730
That is, they want to have
a pipeline, you see, of interns,

00:09:59.731 --> 00:10:03.950
to have a relationship with professors who
are working on subjects that interest them,

00:10:04.205 --> 00:10:06.526
because you are a good student
who could come and work for us,

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:08.550
and teach us how
to use your methods, etc.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:12.570
So, it's a really good
reason for the relationship.

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:18.610
And in the United States, doctoral
students, for example,

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:22.250
so there is no equivalent
to the numbers that there are in France.

00:10:22.251 --> 00:10:25.190
where doctoral students
can do this on-site, in industry.

00:10:25.191 --> 00:10:26.431
There is no such thing in the United States.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.010
But on the other hand, the three months of summer,

00:10:29.365 --> 00:10:30.946
students can
do whatever they want.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.990
So, they are not tied to the university.

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:35.950
And most of them will do
internships in industry.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:40.610
And so, they are a bit like
the bees of American technology.

00:10:40.611 --> 00:10:43.070
Because you need to circulate
ideas, techniques.

00:10:43.464 --> 00:10:45.450
And one of the things you are also known for,

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:50.110
is that you created the Lab Le
Faire, Facebook AI Research.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:52.470
You are trying to promote the American model, there.

00:10:52.471 --> 00:10:55.490
But no, and in fact,
honestly, today,

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:57.350
I don't know, half of the ecosystem,

00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.870
well, a good part of
the French ecosystem in AI,

00:11:00.029 --> 00:11:00.870
are from Fer.

00:11:00.871 --> 00:11:02.950
Anyway, there is a, clearly, Fer mafia.

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:07.331
So, there are quite a few researchers
and engineers who have passed through Fer,

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:09.910
who then went on to
found boxes, etc.

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:14.310
or began to
work in other labs.

00:11:14.609 --> 00:11:20.310
But there is also the fact that fer
had a cruising speed until last year.

00:11:20.959 --> 00:11:23.830
it had about forty doctoral
students in residence.

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:28.290
So, since the doctorate lasts three years,

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:31.510
that means there were about twelve each year,

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:33.690
who had completed their doctorate at Fer

00:11:34.575 --> 00:11:37.531
with access to computing resources, etc.

00:11:37.532 --> 00:11:40.070
with access to the best
researchers in the field and everything.

00:11:40.071 --> 00:11:44.650
And so, all these people, of course, have completely disrupted the ecosystem.

00:11:44.651 --> 00:11:47.830
I think that the creation
of FAIR Paris in 2015.

00:11:48.529 --> 00:11:51.650
had a huge effect
on the French ecosystem,

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:53.790
finally, Parisian, in particular

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:55.590
French, in general and in Europe, in fact.

00:11:55.650 --> 00:12:01.010
So, it gave ideas
to talented young students

00:12:01.609 --> 00:12:04.750
that, in fact, to do a doctorate in AI,

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:06.990
it's the coolest thing they could imagine.

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:09.330
Fifteen years ago, the coolest thing that people were doing

00:12:09.331 --> 00:12:11.670
young students
who were graduating from school,

00:12:12.115 --> 00:12:13.235
it's about doing the finances.

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:14.570
And that has changed.

00:12:14.945 --> 00:12:16.790
It's no longer about finance
now, it's about AI.

00:12:16.814 --> 00:12:19.731
We had received Neil Zeghidour, who told us they wanted to do a thesis

00:12:19.859 --> 00:12:21.586
and in short, they hadn't necessarily been taken,

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:23.850
but he was told, listen, there is
this thesis... No, no, it's the opposite.

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:26.661
They wanted to write a thesis on
neural networks, at the time,

00:12:26.685 --> 00:12:29.470
and there were so few, it hadn't even returned to Hype,

00:12:29.625 --> 00:12:32.206
what they told him, well, listen,
if you're really interested, go ahead.

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:36.610
Then, finally, actually, it was...
You, you had seen that 40 years ago, but...

00:12:36.611 --> 00:12:39.430
Yes, no, but it's true
that there was a period phase

00:12:39.431 --> 00:12:42.690
for the neuron zone between the end
of the 1980s and the mid-1990s.

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:45.210
Then, everyone
lost interest.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:46.430
It became a bit taboo.

00:12:46.915 --> 00:12:49.370
And it has somewhat
reappeared at the end of the 2000s

00:12:49.371 --> 00:12:53.730
because of the media battle we had
with Yoshua Bengio and Geoffrey Hinton.

00:12:54.855 --> 00:12:55.830
The deep learning, you know.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:58.930
We renamed this deep learning and
then we mainly proposed new methods.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:00.710
Then we finally made it work too.

00:13:00.924 --> 00:13:05.790
And so, it finally convinced the
communities initially, speech recognition,

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:08.070
then, image recognition, of course.

00:13:08.515 --> 00:13:09.515
And then, thirdly,

00:13:10.719 --> 00:13:12.759
the understanding of
natural language, what, NLP,

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:16.810
that deep learning, was
really something that worked.

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:21.710
So, it revolutionized these three fields.

00:13:22.145 --> 00:13:26.350
But it's true that in France,
in the academic environment, in 2015,

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:29.950
there were practically no one doing it in universities.

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:33.171
They were still... Academics
are a bit conservative.

00:13:33.195 --> 00:13:35.510
And then, in France, we like
maths and deep learning,

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:36.786
it's not easy to
do math, you know.

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:39.450
It's not easier if we use methods, like kernel methods,

00:13:39.879 --> 00:13:40.590
Bayesian stuff, all that.

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:41.890
One can do beautiful math.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:44.530
It doesn't work as well, but...
Well, we can write books.

00:13:44.531 --> 00:13:45.170
But it's beautiful math, you know.

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:45.790
But these are beautiful mathematics.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:48.910
How is it that this has become a bit obsolete,

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:53.150
but that there was less interest starting from the early 2000s?

00:13:53.151 --> 00:13:54.790
No, it's rather the mid-1990s.

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:55.810
Mid-1990s, what is it due to?

00:13:55.811 --> 00:13:57.330
It started to decline in 1996.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:13:59.090
Because it was too complicated?

00:13:59.091 --> 00:14:00.310
Did it require too much power?

00:14:00.311 --> 00:14:01.750
There are many reasons.

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:06.170
I'm not the best person to analyze
that, because I was right in the middle of it,

00:14:06.779 --> 00:14:09.270
but I think there are
a combination of reasons.

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:11.510
The first, is that
it is indeed complicated

00:14:11.511 --> 00:14:16.264
and there were no tools
software, portable, etc.

00:14:16.344 --> 00:14:17.510
that people used.

00:14:17.795 --> 00:14:23.530
Everyone used completely different computer platforms.

00:14:23.929 --> 00:14:28.146
You couldn't do a neural network on PCs, it's not possible.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:29.830
It was either Sun workstations,

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:31.090
be it Silicon Graphics,

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:33.530
soit des choses comme
cela, des propriétaires.

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:37.010
There is IRIX, AIX, HPUX,
 all that, etc.

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:41.330
So, we were under Sun, then
we switched to Silicon Graphics, after,

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:43.711
because they had a better performance in terms of time.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:45.710
And there wasn't the software,
there wasn't Python,

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:49.031
there weren't all those gadgets open
source that we could use now.

00:14:49.235 --> 00:14:50.942
And the only open source
tool that there was, were things

00:14:50.943 --> 00:14:54.110
like GCC, Emacs and others,
but not really for that.

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:56.339
So, actually, we, with Léon, had written

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.330
a complete system
with a LISP interpreter,

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:02.070
all at once with
a LISP compiler and all that.

00:15:02.595 --> 00:15:05.191
But we couldn't
really exchange our software.

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:08.330
There was no platform,

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.650
there was no SourceForge,
there was no GitHub,
there was no Internet.

00:15:10.651 --> 00:15:13.950
It happened later.

00:15:14.454 --> 00:15:18.214
So, in fact, everyone had to invest

00:15:18.379 --> 00:15:21.611
six months, a year to
create a platform

00:15:21.691 --> 00:15:23.698
software to make neural networks work.

00:15:23.795 --> 00:15:25.530
Most people were making mischief,

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:27.070
because it is very
easy to do silly things

00:15:27.071 --> 00:15:29.330
when implementing backprop for the first time,

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:31.150
we do an hour of sign language or something.

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:34.070
So, it was a big
investment for people.

00:15:34.071 --> 00:15:38.330
And so, most people
did not want to do that.

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:39.690
So, what were they doing?

00:15:39.691 --> 00:15:41.225
They used MATLAB
and then they did something

00:15:41.249 --> 00:15:44.010
simple, but building a neural network with MATLAB,

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:45.771
at the time, that wasn't really possible.

00:15:45.795 --> 00:15:48.010
There really weren't the tools.

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:51.046
Except for people who
were investing the time in the tools.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:52.788
What we did with Léon,
that's what gave us

00:15:52.812 --> 00:15:54.406
super powers, it was that we made our own tools.

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:54.790
Okay.

00:15:55.315 --> 00:15:58.990
And this allowed us to
make convolutional networks,

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:00.450
see how they worked.

00:16:01.789 --> 00:16:04.650
A whole graphical library
and all that, of visualization and everything,

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:06.490
which was actually more powerful than what we have today.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:07.790
In fact, it's amazing.

00:16:09.109 --> 00:16:13.530
And so, it meant that there was no communication

00:16:13.531 --> 00:16:15.550
that there is now,
which is instantaneous,

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:18.309
where you download the latest
code that is associated to

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:21.530
paper and you can make
the last network turn.

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:24.390
There, one had to spend six months, a year,

00:16:24.619 --> 00:16:25.619
to implement something.

00:16:26.449 --> 00:16:27.849
So, it really was necessary to believe in it.

00:16:28.045 --> 00:16:29.605
That means you had the garbage.

00:16:30.204 --> 00:16:34.710
and you would reimplement cleanroom the papers.

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:37.550
There were people who wanted
to reimplement convolutional networks.

00:16:37.690 --> 00:16:38.850
There weren't many.

00:16:40.099 --> 00:16:41.690
And there was this legend that,

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:44.870
you see, I was more or less
the only one who knew how to make them work,

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:45.690
which was completely wrong.

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:47.286
But it wasn't that complicated.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:49.070
But it's true that it
required tools.

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:49.930
At the same time, it's the name.

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:50.630
Yann the qu'un.

00:16:51.029 --> 00:16:52.190
You see, we believe there's only one.

00:16:52.191 --> 00:16:53.250
There's only one.

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:57.050
But that means you had people above you,

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:59.313
in management
at Bell Labs, etc.,

00:16:59.314 --> 00:17:01.650
who believed in it, after all, and who weren't afraid

00:17:01.651 --> 00:17:04.450
to have the means, the time, for all that?

00:17:04.451 --> 00:17:05.829
The way Bell Labs operated.

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:09.410
So, the director of the lab, a
guy named Larry Jackel,

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:12.589
who had created the group in 1985, I think,

00:17:13.989 --> 00:17:16.050
based on neural networks.

00:17:17.484 --> 00:17:18.855
In fact, he's a physicist, by profession.

00:17:18.879 --> 00:17:21.190
And then, he had a new department,

00:17:21.349 --> 00:17:22.629
so he wondered what to do.

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:23.750
Because at Bell Labs,

00:17:24.669 --> 00:17:26.297
we hired intelligent people, we gave them

00:17:26.321 --> 00:17:28.189
the means to succeed,
 and we gave them peace.

00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:29.270
OK.

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:30.110
It's cool, that.

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:32.901
If he was asking for resources,
obviously, he was asking questions.

00:17:32.925 --> 00:17:33.925
But basically,

00:17:34.554 --> 00:17:37.091
we let people work on what they wanted.

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:39.399
So, he went completely off track and asked

00:17:39.479 --> 00:17:40.990
on what I would have worked,
what is the cool thing.

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:43.310
And he met John Hopfield,

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:44.810
who was a professor at Princeton,

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:48.130
but who had a part-time job at Bell Labs.

00:17:48.429 --> 00:17:49.890
It was between Princeton and Caltech.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:52.150
And he asked me
on what I would have worked.

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:53.370
And John Hopfield said to him.

00:17:54.309 --> 00:17:54.970
that he was a physicist,

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:57.991
that he was working on hardware
for neural networks,

00:17:58.274 --> 00:18:00.510
special chips for
making neural networks.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:01.990
This concept is not new.

00:18:02.569 --> 00:18:08.810
John Hopfield, a Nobel laureate in physics with Geoffrey Hinton two years ago

00:18:08.909 --> 00:18:13.450
for what are called Hopfield and Geoffrey
networks, and Boltzmann machines.

00:18:15.159 --> 00:18:16.450
It was originally a biophysicist.

00:18:17.099 --> 00:18:21.010
And so Larry got into that.

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:22.430
He hired a few people.

00:18:22.674 --> 00:18:24.490
And then I came in in 88.

00:18:26.029 --> 00:18:28.290
And then I started working on
convolutional networks pretty quickly.

00:18:28.291 --> 00:18:30.491
because I had already
worked on that in Toronto.

00:18:30.639 --> 00:18:32.750
They had character-based databases.

00:18:33.254 --> 00:18:35.110
And so, in two months, I was there.

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:38.479
I beat all the
performance records

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:41.110
on the
character-based things.

00:18:41.459 --> 00:18:45.870
And there was something, a funny story,

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:49.689
it was that before... When
I was still a post-doc in Toronto,

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:53.350
Larry Jackel asked me «
Listen, you're coming in October,

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:56.970
we'd like to order a computer for you,

00:18:56.971 --> 00:19:00.030
so that you can work when
you arrive, that it's there when you arrive.

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:01.533
What do you use at the moment ? »

00:19:01.534 --> 00:19:03.030
I said « We use a Sun 4,

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:05.330
there's one that just arrived in Toronto,

00:19:05.331 --> 00:19:08.681
it's great, all of that, it's a huge flop, it's hallucinatory. »

00:19:08.682 --> 00:19:13.090
He says « Ok ».

00:19:13.091 --> 00:19:15.070
And then, it was a Sun 4

00:19:15.071 --> 00:19:19.470
which was the central server
of the entire IT department.

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:21.550
Ah, so everyone was on a mainframe?

00:19:21.551 --> 00:19:22.310
It wasn't a mainframe,

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:24.210
that's what we called a mini-computer.

00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:25.110
It is a mini-computer.

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:26.590
Ah, but you mean everyone was involved?

00:19:26.591 --> 00:19:27.726
Everyone would be on it.

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:31.710
You had 30 or 40 doctoral students who were connecting to that thing.

00:19:31.711 --> 00:19:33.731
Not everyone used it for neural networks,

00:19:34.014 --> 00:19:36.930
but it wasn't really...
Was it a timeshare?

00:19:36.931 --> 00:19:39.970
It was a unique system, you connect.

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:41.486
So, that's the project I was working on.

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:45.570
I also had a workstation
Sun, but Sun 3 with a 68000.

00:19:46.750 --> 00:19:47.110
Amazing.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:49.930
So, I said to him, "Okay, Sun 4,
it's the one with the Sparc."

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:51.676
The Sparc processors of Sun.

00:19:51.677 --> 00:19:53.730
And I arrive at Bell Labs.

00:19:54.429 --> 00:19:55.429
and there is a Sun 4,

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:56.700
but just for me !

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:00.786
That means a lot.

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:02.510
That means a lot of
things, the methods we use.

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:05.670
So, well, I'm a bit
skeptical and then I look at Larry.

00:20:05.794 --> 00:20:08.490
And then he says, smiling
"You know, at Bell Labs,

00:20:09.350 --> 00:20:13.076
you don't become famous
by saving money."

00:20:13.077 --> 00:20:15.010
There's a lot to learn from that.

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:17.169
"You don't get famous
by saving money.

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:17.750
Ah, yes, okay.

00:20:18.139 --> 00:20:19.590
It's very interesting.

00:20:19.730 --> 00:20:22.190
So, if we go back a
little to return a little

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:22.990
more contemporary,

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:24.905
but therefore 2013-2023, that is.

00:20:25.185 --> 00:20:26.239
..

00:20:25.131 --> 00:20:25.870
2025, no ?

00:20:25.871 --> 00:20:27.530
2025, it's the era at Meta.

00:20:27.729 --> 00:20:29.050
You received the Turing Award in 2018

00:20:29.051 --> 00:20:31.132
which is the
Fields medal or the
Nobel Prize

00:20:31.156 --> 00:20:33.030
in computer science, let's
just say so.

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:33.910
Right.

00:20:34.270 --> 00:20:34.550
But, so,

00:20:35.110 --> 00:20:39.030
at that time, the World Models, there
were already two hypotheses

00:20:39.031 --> 00:20:40.350
between LLMs and
World Models.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:41.130
Does that make sense?

00:20:41.131 --> 00:20:42.890
Yes, in a way.

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:46.570
Me, for example, I gave a keynote at NIPS.

00:20:46.675 --> 00:20:47.690
It was still called
NIPS at the time,

00:20:47.810 --> 00:20:50.110
not yet NeurIPS, in 2016.

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:55.234
My main message at the
end of the speech was

00:20:55.258 --> 00:20:57.258
The future of AI
is World Models

00:20:57.282 --> 00:21:01.016
So, learning predictive models
of the world, I think, is the key

00:21:01.785 --> 00:21:05.108
And then learning actions
and then optimizing

00:21:05.447 --> 00:21:09.684
intrinsic objective functions
are really the path forward for

00:21:09.708 --> 00:21:11.924
making progress in AI.

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:15.659
They are systems that
are able to predict

00:21:15.684 --> 00:21:18.251
what the effect of an
action on the world will be, etc.,

00:21:18.511 --> 00:21:22.490
that can be learned in an
autonomous way, and then controlled

00:21:22.491 --> 00:21:23.790
by giving them
an objective, etc.

00:21:24.249 --> 00:21:25.890
So, I had this
idea a long time ago.

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:28.111
That's even from 2016, it's from before.

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:31.590
But let's say, I started talking about it
publicly, if you want, at that time.

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:37.110
But the research hadn't been successful to... If you want,

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:39.550
we couldn't really
do that in articles

00:21:39.551 --> 00:21:41.451
because it was
still just an idea.

00:21:41.935 --> 00:21:42.935
And then,

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:44.650
for several years,

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:48.291
I have had research projects both at Meta and at NYU,

00:21:48.469 --> 00:21:50.671
to Meta for
training models,

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:53.650
which could have been
World Models

00:21:53.974 --> 00:21:55.214
through video prediction.

00:21:56.029 --> 00:21:58.070
In fact, it never worked.

00:21:58.850 --> 00:22:03.530
So, we tried to make it work
until, I don't know, 2021,

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:04.670
something like that.

00:22:04.699 --> 00:22:07.310
Generative models
for video prediction.

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:08.230
It never worked

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:09.330
for,

00:22:09.864 --> 00:22:11.250
as a model
of the world, in fact,

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:13.251
to predict what will happen.

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:15.330
Maybe to create
generations of videos,

00:22:15.470 --> 00:22:16.930
but even at that time, it didn't work because we didn't have

00:22:16.931 --> 00:22:18.711
the diffusion models or anything like that.

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:19.810
Therefore,

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:22.690
according to you, because it's just a bad
representation of the world, right?

00:22:22.691 --> 00:22:23.210
No,

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:28.959
it's the fact that most of the
information in a video is unpredictable.

00:22:29.735 --> 00:22:31.990
The bulk of the information in
a video is unpredictable.

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:33.110
If I take,

00:22:34.630 --> 00:22:37.331
here, the people who are watching
see only a small part of the room,

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:42.331
they may have a vague idea of the surroundings if you turn the camera,

00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:45.190
but you cannot predict the details, you cannot say that,

00:22:45.550 --> 00:22:46.450
outside the camera's field of view,

00:22:46.570 --> 00:22:50.330
there is a video game that is in the process of... little dinosaurs dancing.

00:22:50.331 --> 00:22:51.390
There is a T-Rex, etc.

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:53.411
There are all the people with security here.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:57.251
There is no way you can
predict all the details at the pixel level

00:22:57.349 --> 00:22:59.971
of what is in the video if
the camera starts to turn.

00:23:00.069 --> 00:23:01.190
Who could have predicted?

00:23:01.191 --> 00:23:04.870
The vast majority of the information in a video

00:23:04.871 --> 00:23:08.050
or in any
real-world signal

00:23:08.199 --> 00:23:09.199
is unpredictable.

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:10.450
So, if we try to train

00:23:10.899 --> 00:23:14.490
a generative model to hallucinate
that is not predictable,

00:23:15.164 --> 00:23:17.251
it doesn't work
very well at the end.

00:23:17.349 --> 00:23:19.690
And therefore, the crucial idea

00:23:19.889 --> 00:23:22.290
on which we are working for
world models called JEPA,

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:24.051
Joint Intermediate
Predictive Architecture,

